/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  membrane26_class_sulphate_removal -- a membrane chosen by WHAT IT DOES.

  THE TASK.  A brackish water (3 g/L NaCl, 2 g/L MgSO4) must lose its
  sulphate and may keep its sodium chloride.  Before choosing a product, a
  student chooses a FUNCTION: `module NF_DIVALENT_SELECTIVE;` names a
  functional CLASS (data/standards/assets/NF_DIVALENT_SELECTIVE.dat, kind
  membraneClass) -- "high sulphate retention with NaCl passage".

  WHAT THE CLASS IS.  An ILLUSTRATIVE teaching profile, not a membrane
  anyone sells and not a measurement: an OBSERVED rejection per salt at its
  own reference test (2,000 ppm, 4.8 bar gauge, 25 C, 15 % recovery) --
  NaCl 30 %, MgSO4 95 %, each the middle of the class's screening window
  (10-50 %, > 90 %) -- a water permeance (10 L m-2 h-1 bar-1) and the
  generic element it is deployed in (Generic-4040, by reference).  The run
  says so on every line that matters: `[illustrative]`, and the element's
  own `[estimate]` lines.

  WHAT THE ENGINE DERIVES.  `constantRejection` needs the INTRINSIC
  rejection, against the wall; the class states the OBSERVED one, against
  the feed.  `fromRatedTest` runs THIS element at the class's reference
  test and finds the R_int whose predicted observed rejection is the
  class's (src/unitOperations/membrane/RatedTestInversion):
      R_int(NaCl)  = 0.37566  (KPI R_int_fromRatedTest_NaCl)
      R_int(MgSO4) = 0.96342  (KPI R_int_fromRatedTest_MgSO4)

  THE ANSWER, three elements in series at 7 bar absolute:
      water recovery 32.45 %, permeate 811 kg/h;
      R_obs(NaCl)  0.2865 -> permeate 2140 ppm NaCl   (feed 3000 mg/L)
      R_obs(MgSO4) 0.9466 -> permeate  107 ppm MgSO4  (feed 2000 mg/L),
                             i.e. 85 ppm as sulphate.
  The sodium chloride passes, the sulphate stays: what the class was chosen
  to do.  Both observed rejections sit a little below the class's stated
  ones (30 %, 95 %): at 7 bar a third of the water leaves, so the bulk
  concentrates along the vessel, which the reference test at 15 % recovery
  barely saw.

  WHAT A STUDENT SHOULD CHANGE.
    * The CLASS: `module NF_OPEN;` passes more salt of every kind (it rates
      NaCl only, so MgSO4 then needs a typed R_int with its source --
      a class says nothing about a salt it does not rate); `module
      NF_TIGHT;` holds more NaCl too.
    * The PRESSURE and the number of ELEMENTS: recovery against permeate
      sulphate.
    * The CLASS's assumptions themselves: copy the record into this case's
      constant/assets/ and move its MgSO4 rejection inside its band -- the
      intrinsic value is re-derived; typing a number here instead is
      refused, because a rated salt's intrinsic rejection is derived.
    * Then the PRODUCT: a commercial element (`module NF270-4040;`, its data
      sheet's rated test) -- the shortlist from a class to products is a
      later slice (DEV.md 4c C189 (5)).

  NOT a validation: no number in this case is measured.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        NF;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( retentate  permeate );
        operation
        {
            module           NF_DIVALENT_SELECTIVE;   // a functional CLASS (illustrative), data/standards/assets/
            transport        constantRejection;       // the one law a class can run
            transportParameters
            {
                rejection { NaCl fromRatedTest;  MgSO4 fromRatedTest; }
                source    "DERIVED: the illustrative class NF_DIVALENT_SELECTIVE's observed reference rejections (NaCl 30 %, MgSO4 95 %) inverted with this element's own film at the class's reference tests";
            }
            elements         3;                 // Generic-4040 elements in series in one vessel
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            nNodes           20;                // stations per element

            massTransfer
            {
                model          SchockMiquel;    // coefficients + window: the element's spacer record
                viscosity      0.89e-3;         // Pa.s, water at 25 C
                diffusivity    1.2e-9;          // m2/s, ONE value for both lumped salts (NaCl 1.6, MgSO4 0.85 x 1e-9)
            }
            pressureDrop
            {
                model          SchockMiquel;
            }
            polarisation
            {
                suctionCorrection  GeraldesAfonso2006;
                ionCoupling        none;          // lumped salts: nothing to couple
            }
        }
    }
);
